{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_202605061802205db2008253ad45ab","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.1","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The `reminderProvider` is `@escaping () -> (String, String)`. Since `ContextEngine` is `@MainActor`, calling `generate()` from this non-isolated closure is the problem. The error message \"cannot find 'ContextEngine' in scope\" is misleading - it's actually a concurrency isolation issue.\n\nLet me try removing `@MainActor` from ContextEngine. The original issue was that ContextEngine accessed `CalendarService.shared` which is `@MainActor`. But the static `shared` property access itself is fine - it's the method calls that need main actor. Let me check...\n\nActually, `CalendarService` is `@MainActor`, so accessing its properties and methods requires MainActor isolation. If ContextEngine is not `@MainActor`, it can't call `calendarService.generateCalendarReminders()`.\n\nThe simplest fix is to make the closure capture the call properly. I can:\n1. Remove `@MainActor` from ContextEngine and make the problematic calls use `MainActor.assumeIsolated` or dispatch\n2. Keep `@MainActor` on ContextEngine but wrap the call in RemindMeApp\n\nActually, let me try a different approach. The real issue is that the `reminderProvider` closure is called from a Timer/background context. I should make the closure call `generate()` on the main actor.\n\nBut actually, the simplest approach: remove `@MainActor` from ContextEngine. The `CalendarService.shared` reference is fine to access. The `CalendarService` methods will be called - and since this code will be called from a Timer (not main thread), I need to handle the MainActor requirement.\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-think. The initial build of ContextEngine WITHOUT `@MainActor` succeeded. The issue is specifically when RemindMeApp.swift tries to reference it.\n\nLet me try removing `@MainActor` and see what error we get. The issue might be something else entirely - maybe a Swift concurrency issue that prevents the module from being compiled properly.\n\nActually, I think the real issue is simpler. Let me remove `@MainActor` from ContextEngine and see if the build error changes. If ContextEngine calls `@MainActor` CalendarService methods, Swift 5.9 in strict concurrency mode might produce errors.\n\nLet me try it.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"33d24eb8-fca9-4f18-9721-28897c4bbc1b","uuid":"89048c73-6d5f-489f-9913-47a81fec21cd"}
